  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure centered on the mid Atlantic coast extends back across \nthe Ohio valley while remaining the primary weather influence in SE \nMi today. As has been the case the last few mornings, fog is once \nagain a factor leading up to sunrise with a slightly different \nfootprint each night depending on boundary layer wind direction. The \nSE component this morning has a plume augmented by Lake Erie and St \nClair moisture moving inland leading up to forecast issuance time. \nHi-res model wind guidance indicates continued veering of the \nboundary layer flow that favors a shift toward the north and east \nduring the early morning. The Dense Fog Advisory factors this in \nalthough with a chance for additional refinement on areal coverage \nbased on observations leading up to sunrise. \n\nConditions otherwise exhibit thickening mid and high clouds moving \nin ahead of the Midwest frontal system. The leading low to mid level \nmoisture axis is also producing pockets of radar returns from NW \nLower Mi back into southern WI. Model depictions of theta-e \nadvection are modest over top of dry air below 700 mb leaving little \nmore than virga indications through the day. High temperatures near \n70 are the weather highlight, averaging 10 to 15 degrees above \nnormal for late October. \n\nThe surface cold front is slightly more productive as it enters \nnorthern Lower Mi this evening with the chance for a sprinkle \ngrazing Saginaw Bay and northern Thumb by sunrise Monday. Even that \ntends to fade as the low level frontal zone settles farther south \nfrom mid level short wave and upper level jet support that is well \nnorth in Canada. The system lacks moisture but does carry in enough \ncooler air to drop temperatures back down toward more typical late \nOctober readings in the upper 50s and lower 60s Monday afternoon and \nlows in the mid 30s to around 40 by Tuesday morning.   \n\nThe mid to late week period is set for resumption of above normal \ntemperatures as low pressure organizes across the Plains and central \nCanada. The early week Great Lakes front stalls in the Ohio valley \nand is then pulled back north across the area by the Plains to \nMidwest low pressure system. Solutions among the 25/00Z models are \nonce again lean on QPF due to limited forcing this far east along \nthe warm front. Prospects for rain look better along the trailing \ncold front expected in Lower Mi late Thursday into Friday.",
